  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 310 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nA variety of weather is in store for the Tri-State area through \nSunday. The long term period will start off wet and windy until \nsunny skies return Friday morning. Temperatures will be consistent \nwith high temperatures forecast in the 60s to 70s Wednesday through \nSunday.\n\nWednesday morning, our region is forecast to remain in a mostly \nsouthwest upper-level flow with a jet streak overhead. This jet \nstreak is expected to remain over the region through Thursday \ncreating windy afternoons. Gusts from 30 to 45 mph are possible\nfor the area, with our Colorado counties being on the higher \nend. There is also a cut off low in southwest CONUS that is \nforecast to traverse east that will eventually take us out of \nour rainy pattern. Models are showing anywhere from 1-3 inches \nof rain Wednesday through Thursday evening with a band of \nlocalized heavier rainfall setting up somewhere in the county\nwarning area (CWA). The speed and track of the upper-level cut \noff low and where a surface low sets up in western Kansas will \nultimately determine precipitation amounts. Severe weather is \nnot anticipated due to lack of instabilty in the environment. \nLapse rates are shallow, shear is weak and CAPE is lacking. \nWednesday evening would be our best chance for weak \nthunderstorms based on GFS ensembles showing <10% chance of CAPE\nvalues greater than 500 J/kg. Low confidence (<10%) that any \nweak thunderstorms are able to form. Models are in a bit of \ndisagreement on how quickly this low will move on, but rainy \nconditions for the CWA are forecast to end sometime Thursday \nevening.\n\nFriday, sunny skies return to the CWA as we enter a weak \nnorthwesterly to zonal upper-level pattern. Precipitation is \nnot expected as Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are less \nthan 5% for the Tri- State area. A weak jet streak may set up \nover the area Friday and remain through the weekend. This could \ncause some windy conditions for our Colorado counties with \nafternoon gusts around 30 mph possible. Fire weather is not a \nconcern at this time due to steady precipitation expected \nthrough the week and relative humidity (RH) values generally \nforecast over 30%.",
